Created this blog. Will probably abandon it, knowing me. Adult life, psychiatric challenges, Claude (I'm ashamed to admit), and plain neglect have led to the severe atrophy of my programming skills. This blog is documenting my journey to redevelop them. This GitHub account meant to create public accountability and proof-reminder of (re)learning for myself. 

I genuinely hate LLMs so much. I've done multiple years of research on ANNs (with 2 papers to show for it. yay.) and think they're very impressive in specific domains where high-dimensional but certain patterns exist. For general purpose they're absolute dogshit. I've never thought they were good at code and I'm thoroughly aware from my research how they plateau and forget and break. Unfortunately, my last year at anonymous university had a curriculum mandate for LLM coding on a lot of projects. It's kind of like a drug in the same way that a Facebook feed is like a drug. Having 90% shitty output actually makes our brains go feral for that 10% of the time it actually works. I hated what it did to my brain. I tried just using AI as a search engine for a while, but then noticed that whenever I got curious about something or stuck on finding an answer, I developed a habit of reaching for my phone. These two things terrified the shit out of me, and I immediately swore off any AI for anything ever. I deleted my accounts and blocked the websites. I'm genuinely ashamed that I ever used these tools at all, especially considering how fucking bad they are at their job. I was ashamed every time I opened them. I've never not hated the technology or myself for using it, but peer pressure and that evolutionary craving to offload as many cognitive resources as possible got to me. I should have been stronger, no matter how tired I was at the time. At the end of the day, shame doesn't fuel growth, so I'm going to leave this post here as a final monument to it and just take pride in never using these tools again going forward. As an aside, I've never used them for anything other than code (only when mandated for class or for a few one-off scripts) or searching. I would rather die than lean on AI for writing or idea generation - anyone who does that is creatively bankrupt and I give them no grace. Regardless, I only hope that I'll move past this chapter of my coding journey gracefully, and haven't permanently fried my ability to write respectable code. I would say that even bad human code is better than good LLM code, as is true for art, but fortunately for me good LLM code doesn't exist (if you think it does, I hate to break it to you but you're a terrible programmer).

Anyway, as I said, I will probably forget this blog exists tomorrow. I have severe ADHD, so yeah. If anyone on earth is reading this, have a lovely day.
